@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — registered transmissions: western esotericism and European
# vernacular practice
#
# All at pm:corpusNamed. The vernacular strands are the interesting half:
# their contact routes are decided by the date and the hand of the written
# fixation, and that question is open for every one of them.
#

# pmt:ArsNotoria has left this file: passages have been read and the instance
# now stands in traditions/ars-notoria.ttl at pm:placesEntered. The contact
# route is unchanged at documented.
#
# The registration said this was one of the few pre-modern western claims
# formulated so that it could fail, with a named capacity, a named period and
# a named regimen. The reading confirms all three and adds the one thing that
# decides how far they carry: the witness that is in print, Turner's
# translation and the Latin of the Agrippa text alike, omits the notae, and
# the notae are what the text itself calls the centre of the art. The regimen
# is therefore documented and the procedure is not. That stands on the
# instance there. See prima-materia#525.

# The Solomonic grimoire transmission was registered here and was worked off
# pm:corpusNamed on 2026-09-12. Its instance and its nodes now stand in
# traditions/solomonic-grimoire.ttl; nothing of it is left here, because a
# tradition typed in two files is a collision the uniqueness guard catches and
# a tradition described in two files is a drift no guard catches.

# pmt:RenaissanceMagic has left this file: nine places of Agrippa's three books
# were read on 2026-09-22 and the instance now stands in
# traditions/renaissance-magic.ttl at pm:placesEntered. The contact route is
# unchanged at documented, and for the reason the registration already gave —
# this is a compilation whose author names his sources, the Spanish Picatrix
# among them, so an agreement with the Arabic astral magic is reception with a
# route and not a second witness.
#
# The registration's remark about Dee's diaries stands unchanged and unused:
# they are a working log with dates, which makes them a different kind of
# witness from the treatises around them, and they are still unopened. The note
# is carried over to the work identifier in the new file so that it is not lost
# with this entry.

pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Graeco-Egyptian alchemy"@en, "Griechisch-ägyptische Alchemie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The earliest alchemical transmission, in which the operation on matter is reported together with dreams that interpret it."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zosimos of Panopolis, the Visions and the authentic memoirs" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented .

pmt:ArabicAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Arabic alchemy"@en, "Arabische Alchemie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the balance, of elixirs and of the generation of life by art, carried under the name of Jābir by a corpus larger than one man."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Jābirian corpus; al-Rāzī, Sirr al-Asrār" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented .

# pmt:LatinAlchemy has left this file: the Turba philosophorum, the first of the
# three works its registration named, has been read complete at the wording and
# the instance now stands in traditions/latin-alchemy.ttl at pm:placesEntered,
# with its contact route unchanged at documented. The registration's judgement
# held on both counts. The double reading is in the work and not imposed on it —
# the same volume gives day-counts for matter in a vessel and makes the whole
# outcome turn on the operator's patience — and the corpus does state what the
# examinability-driven entrance of SPEC §14 asks for: nine cautions, five
# prerequisites of stated necessity, and two acts of assay with a criterion. The
# one thing the registration did not anticipate is what the run found missing:
# the work never states a way of knowing whether a material is what it is
# called, and the Summa perfectionis, which is reported to carry that catalogue,
# has been added to the corpus there as an unopened work.

pmt:ChristianTheosophy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Christian theosophy"@en, "Christliche Theosophie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Böhme's transmission of a divine self-disclosure through opposition, reported as having been seen rather than reasoned."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Böhme, Aurora; Böhme, De signatura rerum" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented .

pmt:Rosicrucian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Rosicrucian transmission"@en, "Rosenkreuzerische Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission begun by three anonymous manifestos that announced an order and a knowledge, and that produced both by being read."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition whose founding documents describe a body that did not exist before them. Of interest to the project precisely as a documented case of a transmission created by its own announcement."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Fama Fraternitatis; Confessio Fraternitatis; Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented .

pmt:Swedenborgian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Swedenborgian transmission"@en, "Swedenborgische Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A transmission resting on one man's sustained reports of conversation with the dead and with angels, kept as a dated journal over decades."@en ;
    skos:note "Quantitatively the largest body of first-person reports available to the project, and dated throughout, which makes internal consistency checkable in a way most such material is not."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented .

# pmt:NorseSeidr has left this file: the Hávamál, the fourth chapter of Eiríks
# saga rauða and the sixth and seventh of Ynglinga saga have been read at the
# wording, and the instance now stands in traditions/norse-seidr.ttl at
# pm:placesEntered. Its contact route is unchanged at disputed, and the note
# this file carried about the recording hand is borne out rather than removed:
# the séance is framed as superstition by the man who records it, and the
# frame is what makes the condition inside it worth having.

# pmt:InsularCeltic has left this file: the glossary entry on imbas forosnai,
# the prologue to the Senchas Már, the verse tract on the glám dícenn and the
# Welsh chapter of Giraldus have been read at the wording, and the instance now
# stands in traditions/insular-celtic.ttl at pm:placesEntered. Its contact
# route is corrected from disputed to documented, and the correction is the
# finding: everything that could be read reaches us inside a Christian Latin
# frame the witnesses themselves display, and two of them name Patrick as the
# authority that abolished the procedures they describe. What the registration
# promised is borne out — the prohibition stands in the same paragraph as the
# procedure — and what the reading adds is the satire whose stated outcome
# falls on the poets if the poets were the ones in the wrong.

# pmt:FinnicIncantation has left this file. Seven sections of Abercromby's
# translation of Lönnrot's collection of 1880 have been read at the wording and
# the instance now stands in traditions/finnic.ttl at pm:placesEntered. The
# contact route stays disputed: the translator's judgement that the origin
# passage has no counterpart outside Finnish and Estonian is a comparison made
# in 1898 and is recorded as his, not entered as a claim. What the corpus of the
# registration named, Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot, was not opened — it has no
# public-domain English translation, and the collection that does is one whose
# compiler gives no place of recording.

pmt:BalticSlavicCharm a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Baltic and Slavic charm transmission"@en, "Baltische und slawische Besprechungstradition"@de ;
    skos:definition "The eastern European transmission of spoken charms for healing, protection and binding, fixed in writing only by nineteenth-century collectors."@en ;
    skos:note "The late fixation is the whole difficulty: the corpus is a collection and not a work, and the project has no attestation mode for that yet."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The published zagovory collections; the Latvian dainas collections" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided .

# pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic has left this file: passages have been read and the
# instance now stands in traditions/european-folk-magic.ttl at
# pm:placesEntered. The contact route is unchanged at documented, which for
# this transmission was never in doubt.
#
# The registration defined the tradition by what its handbooks are for —
# healing, protection, the recovery of stolen goods. The reading moved the
# definition to what they carry beside that and what almost nothing else in
# this graph carries: the conditions under which the procedure is said to be
# spoiled, and in one place a count of applications with its failure left in.
# See prima-materia#513.
